  "reason": "Each normative FR-08 behaviour maps to an assertion that fails if it is violated. Graceful drain: test_fr08.py:104 requires 'interrupted' and :105 bounds drain time, so an unbounded drain fails; :129 requires 'done', so an immediate cancel fails; :159 checks TASKQ_DRAIN_TIMEOUT. Concurrency cap: :202, :223 and :312 check peak_running, which executor.py increments inside the semaphore, so removing the cap pushes the peak above 2 or 3; :158 checks TASKQ_MAX_CONCURRENT. Timeout kill: :181 requires 'timeout', :182 and :108 require no live pid, :183 requires a negative returncode (killed), and :184 requires returncode to be set, which means wait() was awaited. Cancellation: :250 fails if CancelledError is swallowed, and :252-255 check the slot is released and the subprocess killed. Weakness: no test directly asserts the use of asyncio.TaskGroup or asyncio.wait_for. Only their observable behaviour is pinned.",
